The devices listed above are installed on your system and depend on Option ROM.
If these are plug-and-play extension cards, it is near certain that they have
been signed with Microsoft's keys (older devices may also not have been signed
at all). As we will enroll Microsoft's Secure Boot keys, proceeding should cause
no issues with these devices.

If you are on a laptop or other OEM hardware, these could have been signed with
keys other than Microsoft's. If your BIOS allows wiping the Secure Boot keys,
that is a good sign that all hardware included with the device is *intended* to
run with no keys or only Microsoft's keys enrolled. This again would mean that
enrolling custom keys as well as Microsoft's (as done by this script) is
sufficient to have them functioning properly.

It is possible to dump the Option ROM and check its signature to make sure it
has actually been signed with Microsoft's keys. This is a safe but rather
involved step, as it requires manually building parts of Intel's EDK2 (EFI
Development Kit 2). This script will take care of the process but be aware that
it may take some time. You will also be prompted for some visual confirmation as
not all parts of the process can be automated reliably and/or securely.
